  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to the specialty pharmacy program",
    "basePrintNo": "A8826",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A8826-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "ASSEMBLY",
       "districtCode": 75,
       "fullName": "Richard Gottfried",
       "imgName": "527_richard_gottfried.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 527,
       "sessionMemberId": 321,
       "sessionYear": 2009,
       "shortName": "GOTTFRIED"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §367-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8826\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the social services law, in relation to\nthe specialty pharmacy program\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: This bill would exempt any pharmacy\nowned and operated by a not-for-profit organization solely for the\npurpose of providing drugs to individuals diagnosed with cystic fibrosis\nas part of a comprehensive clinical care program approved by the\nnational organization that accredits cystic fibrosis care centers\nprovided such not-for-profit organization maintains a national registry\nof cystic fibrosis clinical data shall be exempt from the specialty\npharmacy program.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: Cystic fibrosis is an inherited chronic disease that\naffects the lungs and digestive system of about 30,000 children and\nadults in the United States (70,000 worldwide). A defective gene and its\nprotein product cause the body to produce unusually thick, sticky mucus\nthat clogs the lungs and leads to life-threatening lung infections; and\nobstructs the pancreas and stops natural enzymes from helping the body\nbreak down and absorb food. In the 1950s, few children with cystic\nfibrosis lived to attend elementary school.  Today, advances in research\nand medical treatments have further enhanced and extended life for chil-\ndren and adults with CF. The predicted age of survival for a person with\nCystic Fibrosis is now more than 37 years of age. Many children and\nyoung adults with CF take over twenty medications per day including four\norally inhaled medications with treatment times exceeding three hours\nper day.\n\nThe Cystic Fibrosis Foundation has, since its inception in the 1950s,\nprovided pharmacy services to patients as part of a comprehensive clin-\nical support program which includes CF care centers across the state.\n\nCF Services is the largest provider of pharmacy services, including\nspecialty medications, for the state's CF Medicaid patients, serving 40%\nof those with the disease. Under the current criteria for the Specialty\nPharmacy Program, CF Services is ineligible to participate as it does\nnot and cannot provide the number and range of drugs required. The CF\npharmacy only provides drugs exclusively for the treatment of Cystic\nFibrosis.\n\nIn fact, if enacted the specialty pharmacy program as recently proposed\nwould exclude the CF pharmacy which threatens the continuity and coordi-\nnation of care. People with CF are routinely more on and off the Medi-\ncaid rolls as their income fluctuates. The CF pharmacy provides a safety\nnet for those who lose their Medicaid eligibility so they do not also\nlose access to their critical medications. CF Services provides emergen-\ncy supplies of medications and works with patients to find other cover-\nage. This allows New York Medicaid patients who lose eligibility to\ncontinue treatment and keeps them out of the hospital.  This practice\nultimately saves the state money since these same patients are likely to\n\nbecome Medicaid eligible again and will require less care if they are\nable to maintain their health.\n\nThe importance of the CF pharmacy to patients and the coordinated care\nof CF patients cannot be understated. Finally and importantly, the phar-\nmacy continues to provide an effective fulfillment function for the\ndevelopment of new drugs to effectively treat the disease.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: None.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
